{"context":"v1.x distributes framework lifecycle knowledge as adapter binaries built from the Core repository. ADR-009 established the boundary that v2 preserves: the Core owns contracts, framework knowledge lives outside the Core, and the engine communicates with framework executables as standalone subprocesses over a JSON command contract. What v1.x never defines is the framework artifact itself as a governed, independently versioned, lifecycle-managed object — it remains an ad-hoc binary with closed-set discovery, and framework knowledge still leaks into the Core (D1).\n\nThe v2 identity reframes the product: Anvil as a whole is *the* software delivery standard — the system of delivery lifecycle specification and runtime — and each framework artifact is *an* delivery lifecycle standard (ANVIL_V2_TRAN Source: ADR-021-delivery-lifecycle-standard-model.md Date:2026-08-03 Status:Proposed.","decision":"Adopt the delivery lifecycle standard model defined in ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §5. This ADR supersedes ADR-009 in model, governance, and distribution; the subprocess contract boundary established by ADR-009 is preserved.\n\n### 3.1 Three-layer model (§5.1)\n\n```text\nDelivery Lifecycle Specification      ← what a legal lifecycle IS (the authority)\n        │  implemented by\n        ▼\nAnvil Runtime (Core)                    ← the engine: enforces the specification,\n        │  executes                        invokes the standards\n        ▼\ndelivery lifecycle standards         ← framework lifecycle content for one\n        (anvil-standard-*)                 framework\n```\n\n- The **delivery lifecycle specification** defines what a legal lifecycle is; it is owned by Core governance.\n- The **Anvil Run","consequences":"**Positive:** framework lifecycle knowledge becomes a governed, independently versioned asset class; compatibility is declared and negotiated at adoption instead of assumed; new frameworks never require a Core change; the Core's framework-freedom becomes provable (A3, ADR-026).\n\n**Negative:** governance burden increases (mitigated: standards remain executable content under the existing subprocess contract — §11.2); v1.x users and vocabulary must migrate (ADR-028, ADR-032); ADR-009 is superseded and the docs corpus must be revised (plan §7).","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n- **Keep the v1.x adapter model unchanged.** Rejected: no lifecycle states, no compatibility declaration, closed-set distribution (A5); it cannot carry the standard identity (§1.1).\n- **Framework knowledge inside the runtime.** Rejected: contradicts manifesto §3.5 and A3; \"framework knowledge inside the runtime is a defect\" (§5.8).\n- **Separate specification repository.** Rejected by the plan (§5.2): the delivery lifecycle specification stays authored in the Core repository, with an "}